{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test is installed in the same mutation-testing ritual that generates its failure cases, because the guard's existence alters the set of mutations that can be planted without the author noticing.","counterconditions":["If the mutation set is generated by an independent process that cannot see the test","If the author plants mutations mechanically without understanding the code","If the test is written before any mutation framework exists","If the result distribution matches what an independent oracle would produce"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"It is one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record."}],"mechanism":"When a test is written under the 'shown-to-fail-first' installation discipline, the author must plant a mutation that makes the test fail before seeing the test pass. This creates a selection effect: only mutations that are legible to the author's current model of the code get planted. The test therefore constrains the space of mutations that can be observed, rather than independently describing pre-existing behavior.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 1-of-9 compile.py loss under shown-to-fail-first installation constrains rather than describes because the failing test is not measuring the code's behavior but the author's capacity to simulate the reviewer's future self in the same week.","counterconditions":["If the compile.py mutations are generated by a process with no access to author memory","If the test is run before the author knows which mutation was planted","If the same loss rate appears for mutations planted by a different author","If the distribution is stable across multiple independent weeks"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"028950f9751ab59a","quote":"The error was not noise, it was masking: mutations no guard catches were reported as caught."}],"mechanism":"Compile.py sits at the interface between the mutation ritual and the code. When the test is installed after the mutation is planted, the author has already internalized the mutation as a known failure. The test's pass/fail signal is then confounded with the author's memory of having planted that specific mutation. In 8 of 9 cases the author's memory matches the test's verdict; the 1 loss is where memory and execution diverge.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.64},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test describes its subject only when its installation order is decoupled from the mutation it is meant to catch; shown-to-fail-first installation creates a constraint because the test's own existence becomes a variable in the system under test.","counterconditions":["If the mutation would have been planted identically without the test","If the test's assertions are derived from a specification independent of the code","If the same distribution appears when tests are added after mutations","If the test author is blind to the mutation-generation process"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9","quote":"| GAP REG-1 | 0/6 |"}],"mechanism":"In shown-to-fail-first, the test is added to the codebase before the mutation is planted. The mutation is chosen to make the test fail, which means the test's semantics define what counts as a failure. The test is thus part of the causal graph that produces the observed mutation outcomes, not an external instrument measuring them.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.81},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The asymmetry between compile.py (1/9 lost) and the ordinarily-guarded modules (4/4 to 6/7) is not a property of the tests but of the guard installation order: compile.py was installed after the mutation, the others before.","counterconditions":["If compile.py mutations are drawn from the same distribution as module mutations","If the installation order is randomized across the repository","If the 1/9 result replicates in a second independent week","If the ordinarily-guarded modules show the same loss rate when installed after"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record"}],"mechanism":"When a guard is installed before its mutation, the mutation must be crafted to evade the guard, which forces the mutation to be non-trivial and therefore more detectable. When a guard is installed after the mutation (as in shown-to-fail-first), the mutation can be trivial because it only needs to make the test fail once. The 1/9 loss occurs where the test was asked to constrain a mutation that had already been chosen without the test's constraint.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.69},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describes it when the test's counterexamples are drawn from the same authorial act that produced the code, because then the test cannot distinguish between 'the code is correct' and 'the author cannot imagine a counterexample'.","counterconditions":["If the mutations are generated by an adversary with different blind spots","If the test's assertions are mechanically derived from a specification","If the same 3-of-26 rate appears for code written by a different author","If the survivor mutations are found to be semantically distinct from the caught ones"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12","quote":"THE THREE SURVIVORS ARE ONE BUG IN TWO COSTUMES: a guard that checks DECLARED DATA does not see ADDED BEHAVIOUR."}],"mechanism":"In a single-author, single-week repository, the mutations and the tests are both generated by the same model. The model's blind spots are shared across both. The 3-of-26 survivor result is therefore a measure of the model's blind-spot size, not of the code's defect rate. The test constrains the code to the author's imaginative horizon.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.58},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 3-of-26 distribution is a constraint artifact, not a description of code quality, because the test suite was designed to maximize mutation score rather than to capture the semantic difference between declared and added behavior.","counterconditions":["If the surviving mutations are semantically trivial and the caught ones critical","If the test suite is written without reference to mutation scores","If the same 3-of-26 ratio holds for a semantically balanced mutation set","If the survivors are fixed immediately upon discovery without changing the test suite"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12","quote":"a guard that checks DECLARED DATA does not see ADDED BEHAVIOUR."}],"mechanism":"When tests are written under the pressure of a mutation-testing metric, they are optimized to catch the mutations that are easy to catch. The 3 survivors are not the most important defects; they are the ones that fall outside the metric's resolution. The test constrains the code by making the caught mutations unreachable in future edits, while the survivors remain invisible.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.77}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["src-a0a64514712b0c27859f1da10c66db2201d86b59"]},"simulation_proposals":[]}